### What is a shared database?

With a shared database, all services point at the same physical database and the same tables. The immediate appeal is real: ACID transactions spanning entities, SQL joins across domains, one source of truth with no synchronization, and one database to operate.

```java
package com.bank.shared;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class SharedDbTransferService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private final StatementRepository statementRepo;

    public SharedDbTransferService(AccountRepository accountRepo,
                                    StatementRepository statementRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
        this.statementRepo = statementRepo;
    }

    @Transactional // One transaction across both tables
    public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        accountRepo.debit(from, amount);
        accountRepo.credit(to, amount);
        // Same DB: can write statement record in same transaction
        statementRepo.recordTransfer(from, to, amount);
    }
}
```

```text
Shared database advantages:
  One ACID transaction: debit + credit + statement record
  Easy JOIN: SELECT a.name, s.amount FROM accounts a JOIN statements s...
  One source of truth: no synchronization needed
  One DB to operate: backup, monitor, tune
```

### What are the problems with a shared database?

The problems appear as the system and organization grow. The schema becomes a hidden shared contract, there is runtime coupling, and you are forced into one engine.

```text
Shared database problems:

  1. Schema coupling:
     Account table used by: transfer-service, statement-service, fraud-service
     Rename account_number -> acc_num? All three services break!
     Result: lockstep deployments, cross-team coordination

  2. Runtime coupling:
     Statement service runs heavy month-end report query
     -> locks account rows
     -> transfer service blocked for 30 seconds
     -> Rajesh's transfer times out

  3. Single engine:
     Account service needs PostgreSQL (ACID)
     History service needs Cassandra (write scale)
     Both forced onto PostgreSQL with shared DB
```

```java
package com.bank.shared;

// Problem: changing this entity breaks all services that use the table
import jakarta.persistence.Entity;
import jakarta.persistence.Table;
import jakarta.persistence.Column;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {

    // If you rename this column, transfer-service,
    // statement-service, and fraud-service all break
    @Column(name = "account_number")
    private String accountNumber;

    @Column(name = "balance")
    private BigDecimal balance;

    // Getters and setters
}
```

### What is database-per-service?

With database-per-service, each service owns its data exclusively. The owning service is the only one that connects, and others access data through a well-defined API or published events.

```java
package com.bank.account;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@RestController
public class AccountApi {

    private final AccountRepository repository;

    public AccountApi(AccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    // The ONLY way other services access account data
    @GetMapping("/api/accounts/{id}/balance")
    public BigDecimal getBalance(@PathVariable String id) {
        return repository.findBalanceById(id);
    }
}
```

```yaml
# account-service application.yml - own database
spring:
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:postgresql://accounts-db:5432/accounts
    username: account_svc

# statement-service application.yml - separate database
spring:
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:postgresql://statements-db:5432/statements
    username: statement_svc
```

```text
Database-per-service benefits:
  Loose coupling:       schema is private, change freely
  Independent deploy:   migrate one service without coordinating
  Fault isolation:      statement-db slow? transfer-service unaffected
  Polyglot persistence: accounts on PostgreSQL, history on Cassandra
```

### How do you handle cross-service data needs?

Without a shared database, a service that needs another's data either calls its API or keeps a local read model updated by events.

```java
package com.bank.statement;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class AccountEventConsumer {

    private final LocalAccountReadModel readModel;

    public AccountEventConsumer(LocalAccountReadModel readModel) {
        this.readModel = readModel;
    }

    @KafkaListener(topics = "account-events")
    public void onAccountChanged(AccountChangedEvent event) {
        // Statement service keeps its own read copy of account names
        readModel.updateAccountName(
            event.getAccountId(), event.getAccountName());
    }
}
```

```text
Cross-service data access patterns:
  1. API call:    GET /api/accounts/SAV2001 (synchronous, real-time)
  2. Event + local copy: consume AccountChangedEvent, store locally
  3. API composition: aggregate data from multiple APIs at gateway

  Trade-off: eventual consistency in the local copy
             (brief delay after transfer before event is applied)
```

### How do you handle distributed transactions?

A business operation spanning two services becomes a Saga with compensating actions.

```java
package com.bank.saga;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferSaga {

    private final AccountServiceClient accountClient;

    public TransferSaga(AccountServiceClient accountClient) {
        this.accountClient = accountClient;
    }

    public void execute(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Step 1: debit
        accountClient.debit(from, amount);
        try {
            // Step 2: credit
            accountClient.credit(to, amount);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Compensate: reverse the debit
            accountClient.credit(from, amount);
            throw new TransferFailedException(
                "Credit failed, debit reversed", e);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Saga pattern:
  Step 1: debit SAV2001  -> success
  Step 2: credit SAV2002 -> failure!
  Compensate: credit SAV2001 (reverse the debit)
  Result: no money lost, eventual consistency
```

### How do the two approaches compare?

| Aspect | Shared Database | Database-per-Service |
|---|---|---|
| Coupling | Tight (schema is public) | Loose (schema is private) |
| Deployment | Lockstep | Independent |
| Transactions | ACID across entities | Sagas + eventual consistency |
| Joins | SQL joins work | API calls or local copies |
| Fault isolation | None (one DB failure hits all) | Yes (isolated databases) |
| Polyglot | No (one engine) | Yes (best engine per service) |
| Operations | One DB to manage | Many DBs to manage |
| Best for | Small teams, unclear boundaries | Many teams, clear boundaries |

### What is the worst anti-pattern?

The worst of both worlds is splitting the code into microservices but leaving them all on one shared database. You get distributed-system complexity with none of the isolation benefits.

```text
Anti-pattern: microservices + shared database
  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐
  │ Transfer Svc │  │ Statement Svc│  │  Fraud Svc   │
  └──────┬───────┘  └──────┬───────┘  └──────┬───────┘
         │                │                │
         └────────────────┼────────────────┘
                          │
                    ┌─────▼─────┐
                    │ Shared DB │  <- Single point of failure
                    └───────────┘     + tight coupling
                                      + distributed complexity
                                      = worst of both worlds
```

### When should you choose each approach?

Start with a shared database or modular monolith when the domain is small or unclear. Adopt database-per-service when teams and services multiply and independent deployment and fault isolation become worth the added complexity.

```text
Decision framework:
  Small team, few services, unclear boundaries?
    -> Shared database (or modular monolith)
    -> Simpler, faster to build

  Many teams, clear boundaries, need independent deploy?
    -> Database-per-service
    -> Accept Saga complexity for isolation benefits

  Migrating from shared to per-service?
    -> Incremental: extract one service at a time
    -> Use Strangler Fig pattern
    -> Keep shared DB for remaining monolith
```
